{
  "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
  "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question in this case is whether the jury foreperson's announcement that the jury had voted unanimously that Petitioner was not guilty of capital murder and first-degree murder has double jeopardy consequences. We respectfully submit that it does, for two reasons."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is that -- is that altogether clear, because she first said -- didn't she first say that the jury cannot agree on any one charge?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, that's the count. The jury takes lots of votes. Was it utterly clear that the jury could not go back and reconsider that? Unless that was absolutely clear, it seems to me a verdict had not been announced."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does he have a constitutional right to get the answer?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. SLOAN",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And every State court, except for Arkansas here, that has addressed the issue in the context of a hard transition, has said that the defendant does have a right to inquire"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MCDANIEL",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are three key reasons why jeopardy has not terminated in this case. First, the jury was free to revisit capital and first-degree murder when it resumed its deliberations."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MCDANIEL",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Partial verdicts are not contemplated in Arkansas law. It would have been error for him to engage in partial verdicts at that time."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: General McDaniel, how -- how probable is it that when the jury is polled, and having voted unanimously when they were back in the jury room, one of the members of the jury changes his mind and votes the other way? That's not at all probable, is it? But it happens sometimes, doesn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Record",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0002-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the jury foreman said we can't go to negligent homicide because we haven't reached agreement on -- on the manslaughter charge."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Is this a fair explanation of Arkansas law, which doesn't seem to me to be perfectly clear? What the jury is supposed to do is to vote on the greatest offense first and reach a reasonably firm vote. And if they reach a reasonably firm vote that is unanimous not guilty, then they can move on to the next -- to the lower offenses, but that reasonably firm vote is not an absolutely final vote, and there is still the possibility for the jury to go back."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Record",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0002-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the defense counsel said, Judge, I think they ought to be given another Allen charge, and they need to go keep deliberating."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-10-1320",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "b0064ce3217c0132951a54da0e8e2cb54f7b4ec44c9b3514aed39c3db06c3db4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b0064ce3217c-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In conclusion, Your Honor, the jury in this case heard the evidence on both sides, unanimously voted against guilt on the murder charges, announced that in open court, and never said a word that contradicted it."
    },
    {
